  "id": "Compound::DB00482 - Gene::771",
  "verbalization": "Celecoxib, a selective COX-2 inhibitor, is implicated in the reversible hydration of carbon dioxide through its interaction with Ca5a, Ca5b, and Ca6. These calcium-binding proteins are part of the same biological pathway, where they collectively participate in the reversible hydration of CO₂. The evidence suggests that Celecoxib may influence this process by binding to calcium-binding sites, thereby modulating the activity of these proteins. The shared participation of Ca12 in the reversible hydration of CO₂ across all three pathways indicates a potential functional link between Celecoxib and Ca12, possibly through a common mechanism involving calcium-dependent enzymatic activity. The integrated evidence highlights a recurring role of calcium-binding proteins in the regulation of CO₂ hydration, suggesting that Celecoxib may exert its effects by interacting with these proteins, thereby influencing the overall metabolic pathway.",
